  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1158 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nFog has been slow to make progress in the last couple of hours, with \nobservations showing an area of fog over Lincoln County, and another \narea of fog spilling over the CO-WY border over northernmost Weld \nCounty. At this time, a Dense Fog Advisory remains in effect for \nLincoln County until 10 am Sunday morning. Visibilities could drop \nto <1/4 mile at times in the advisory area and adjacent plains. The \nadvisory may need to be expanded if dense fog develops elsewhere. \nWhile we still expect fog to increase in coverage closer to sunrise, \nlow clouds may limit the duration and extent of the lower (<1/4 mi) \nvisibilities. \n\nHow much temperatures warm up Sunday afternoon will be heavily \ndependent on the erosion (or lack thereof) of fog and/or low clouds. \nGiven the trends in model guidance and was has been observed over \nthe last couple of days, fog may be slow to erode over the \nsoutheastern portion of our CWA, with scattered low clouds lingering \ninto the afternoon. As such, high temperatures over areas affected \nby the stubborn low clouds (mainly Lincoln County) may run 5-10 \ndegrees cooler than areas with direct sunshine Sunday afternoon. \nResidual moisture west of the Continental Divide may also lead to \nlow clouds persisting through the late morning for our western \ncounties. \n\nAn upper level ridge to our west will continue to strengthen and \ndrift east over our area on Monday, bringing us temperatures that \nare about 10 degrees warmer than climatology. Afternoon temperatures \non Monday and Tuesday will climb into the low 60s for the Denver \nmetro and east CO plains, with most mountain valleys warming up into \nthe 50s each day as well. Flow aloft turns southerly and strengthens \nahead of the next system on Tuesday afternoon, although the \nstrongest winds now appear to remain just east of our area.\n\nA weakening shortwave move over Colorado on Wednesday, and this will \nbe our next chance for precipitation. As the previous AFD stated, \nthis shortwave will move rather quick, only allowing for a few hours \nof light snow showers at the high elevations. Given the quick nature \nof this system, only a trace of snow accumulation is expected, with \namounts up to 1-2 inches at the highest peaks >12kft. Cross-barrier \nwesterly flow aloft on the backside of this disturbance will also \nencourage a period of downsloping winds late Wednesday morning. Wind \ngusts up to 35 mph could be possible for mountains valleys, with \ngusts as high as 55 mph for ridgelines and areas above the tree \nline. At low elevations, a cold front will push through the northern \nColorado plains and Denver metro sometime late Wednesday with breezy \nnortherly winds behind it. Slightly cooler temperatures should \narrive behind this cold front, although temperatures are still \nlikely to remain a couple of degrees above average. \n\nUnfortunately for snow lovers, both global ensembles and \ndeterministic runs have trended downward in snow amounts with the \nsystem for next weekend. What was previously a potent cutoff low \nis now being depicted as a much weaker, open trough. This weaker \nsolution would only suggest light mountain snow at best Friday \ninto the weekend. However, it is worth noting that some ensemble \nmembers still bring at least one inch of snow to the Denver metro\nsometime next weekend. One possible reason for the whiplash in \nsolutions seems to be that global models keep struggling with the\nlocation of cyclogenesis upstream off the northern Pacific Coast.\nAs has become evident over the last few days, there is a \nconsiderable uncertainty in the forecast beyond day 4, and the \nforecast could still shift in either direction. Decent agreement\nstill exists in temperatures cooling down to seasonal averages \nfor the weekend.",
